  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 212 AM EDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nAnother challenging and very low confidence convection forecast for \ntoday. Latest surface obs and mesoanalysis show frontal boundary \ndraped right along our southern CWA border. North of this front some \npatchy fog has developed but visibilities are highly variable and \nguidance suggests that will continue through the morning as cloud \ncover increases so no plans for a dense fog advisory at this time. \nThis front will slowly lift NE through the early morning as the \nnocturnal LLJ ramps up. Already seeing some convection fire along \nthis front but luckily it remains elevated with virtually no surface-\nbased instability in our area. Will still have to keep an eye on \nthis activity through the early morning though with some low-end \npotential for hail and heavy rain in a region already water-logged. \nHi-res CAM's are struggling immensely to capture this activity and \nsubsequently predict evolution through the early morning. Forecast \nconfidence is low but based on overall mesoscale setup expect this \nactivity to continue and slowly expand NE through the early morning \nas the LLJ intensifies and pushes very high theta-e air back into \nour CWA (850mb dewpoints in excess of 16C). \n\nThere could be a brief lull during the late morning but attention \nthis turns to the potential for another MCS associated with \nconvection currently over SD. Here again confidence is not high but \nhi-res guidance shows a little better agreement in an MCS tracking \nthrough at least our southern half around midday. Unfortunately all \nof the ingredients will be similar to yesterday (very high \ninstability and deep-layer shear) and a damaging squall line is a \npossibility. Slight southward displacement of frontal boundary may \nsupport a focus more toward central IN as reflected in the latest \nSWODY1. However, it should be noted that several hi-res members \ntrack this MCS further north with a track, timing, and intensity \neerily similar to yesterday. Straight-line winds would again be the \nmain concern but hail and an embedded tornado can't be ruled out.\n\nThe other issue will be heavy rain. Placement of the front and \nconvection predicted by several guidance members unfortunately \nsuggest the same area hit hard with heavy rain yesterday could be in \nline to get another round of heavy rain today. Earlier noted \nconvective uncertainties remain but felt confidence enough to extend \nthe flood watch for our southern zones that received significant \nrain yesterday. Ran the flood watch until 03Z to match up with \nneighbors but if the midday convection plays out as expected it \ncould be canceled early.\n\nWith plethora of near-term challenges and uncertainties, did not \nspend a lot of time on the later periods. Unfortunately, this front \nhangs around through at least Fri morning with potential for several \nmore rounds of convection. The front finally does briefly surge \nnorth late Fri into Sat which would bring some temporary relief \nbefore the associated cold front arrives Sunday. Behind this front, \na more lasting period of dry weather is anticipated for early next \nweek.",
